WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.680
As a database engineer,

2
00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:04.200
you'll very often come across
columns in a table that are

3
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.975
filled with duplicates of
data and multiple values.

4
00:00:06.975 --> 00:00:09.000
This can make it quite
challenging to view,

5
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.875
search, and sort your data.

6
00:00:10.875 --> 00:00:13.890
But with the correct
implementation of normalization,

7
00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:15.660
this challenge can
be dealt with.

8
00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.400
By the end of this video,

9
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.200
you'll be able to
explain how to design

10
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.345
a database in a
second normal form,

11
00:00:21.345 --> 00:00:23.775
outline the functional
dependency concept,

12
00:00:23.775 --> 00:00:26.500
and define the partial
dependency concept.

13
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.120
Before we begin, make
sure that you've watched

14
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.375
the video on first
normal form or 1NF.

15
00:00:32.375 --> 00:00:35.390
Database normalization is
a progressive process.

16
00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:37.355
So you must be familiar with 1NF

17
00:00:37.355 --> 00:00:40.060
before you can implement 2NF.

18
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.230
Why do database developers

19
00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:44.405
require database normalization?

20
00:00:44.405 --> 00:00:46.340
If you're going
to store content,

21
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:48.980
you should aim to have the
best possible database.

22
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:52.250
Best means that it is a
proper structure that reduces

23
00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.470
duplication and
ultimately allows for

24
00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:57.130
accurate data analysis
and data retrieval.

25
00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:58.875
To get the best results,

26
00:00:58.875 --> 00:01:00.770
engineers build
tables in a way that

27
00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:02.870
optimizes the
database structure.

28
00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.480
This video focuses on
how to design tables in

29
00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:06.770
a relational database to meet

30
00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:08.950
the second normal form criteria.

31
00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.150
But before you can
learn how to do this,

32
00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:12.740
you need to understand
what is meant by

33
00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.725
the terms functional
and partial dependency.

34
00:01:15.725 --> 00:01:17.720
Functional dependency refers to

35
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.435
the relationship between
two attributes in a table.

36
00:01:20.435 --> 00:01:22.220
The unique value of a column in

37
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.160
a relation determines the
value of another column.

38
00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:26.960
To demonstrate this concept,

39
00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.645
let's take the example
of a table known as R.

40
00:01:29.645 --> 00:01:31.700
This table contains two columns

41
00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:34.090
called X and Y, respectively.

42
00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:35.780
X is a column with a set of

43
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:37.280
unique values which are not

44
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:38.915
replicated elsewhere
in the table,

45
00:01:38.915 --> 00:01:40.825
a primary key, for example.

46
00:01:40.825 --> 00:01:42.710
Y is the column without a set of

47
00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:45.395
unique values like
a non-primary key.

48
00:01:45.395 --> 00:01:47.690
R is the table or relation

49
00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.365
in which the columns
X and Y exist.

50
00:01:50.365 --> 00:01:52.850
Y as a non-primary key with

51
00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:55.760
duplicated values
is dependent on X.

52
00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:57.080
This is because X is

53
00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.550
the table's primary key

54
00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:00.635
as it only contains
unique values.

55
00:02:00.635 --> 00:02:01.910
Don't worry if you don't quite

56
00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:03.560
understand this concept yet.

57
00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:05.450
I'm going to
demonstrate functional

58
00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:07.520
dependency in more detail.

59
00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.130
Let's take the example
of a table called

60
00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.140
Student that holds
key information

61
00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:13.720
on students in a college.

62
00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.915
The table contains
three columns,

63
00:02:15.915 --> 00:02:17.520
a student ID column,

64
00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.460
a name column, and a
date of birth column.

65
00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.370
I need to use this table to find

66
00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.605
the date of birth for
a specific student.

67
00:02:24.605 --> 00:02:26.585
I can't use the name column

68
00:02:26.585 --> 00:02:28.850
because it has
duplicated values.

69
00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:31.205
There are two
students named Tony.

70
00:02:31.205 --> 00:02:33.110
If I query this column,

71
00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:35.360
I'll just receive both
instances of Tony.

72
00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.430
I can't use the date
of birth column

73
00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:38.570
either because there are

74
00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:41.020
two students who share
the same date of birth.

75
00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.860
But I can complete this task

76
00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.870
by using the student ID column.

77
00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.000
All values in this
column are unique,

78
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.060
so it is designated as
the table's primary key.

79
00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:52.415
The values of this
primary key column

80
00:02:52.415 --> 00:02:55.255
determine the information
of the other columns.

81
00:02:55.255 --> 00:02:57.500
This means that each
column in the table is

82
00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:00.140
functionally dependent on
the student ID column.

83
00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:01.970
It's the only column that can be

84
00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:04.130
used to return specific data.

85
00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:05.930
Now that you've
explored the concept

86
00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:07.339
of functional dependency,

87
00:03:07.339 --> 00:03:09.595
let's look at
partial dependency.

88
00:03:09.595 --> 00:03:11.540
Partial dependency refers to

89
00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:14.000
tables with a
composite primary key.

90
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.770
This is a key that consists of

91
00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:18.115
a combination of two
or more columns.

92
00:03:18.115 --> 00:03:21.470
To demonstrate, let's take
the example of a table that

93
00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:22.970
shows the vaccination status of

94
00:03:22.970 --> 00:03:25.145
patients in a hospital database.

95
00:03:25.145 --> 00:03:27.650
The table shows the
vaccination status of

96
00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:29.930
two patients, David and Kate.

97
00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:32.720
It also displays the patient ID,

98
00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.785
vaccine ID, and vaccine name.

99
00:03:35.785 --> 00:03:37.670
There is no one single column

100
00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:39.650
with unique values in each row.

101
00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:41.450
There is no single column that

102
00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.300
can be used as a primary key.

103
00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.350
It's best to combine
both the patient

104
00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:47.330
ID and vaccine ID columns

105
00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:48.920
as a composite primary key to

106
00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.475
create a unique value
in each record.

107
00:03:51.475 --> 00:03:53.630
The vaccination table must meet

108
00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:56.600
the second normal form or 2NF.

109
00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:59.465
All non-key attributes,
the vaccine name,

110
00:03:59.465 --> 00:04:00.950
patient name, and status,

111
00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:03.755
must depend on the
entire primary key value

112
00:04:03.755 --> 00:04:06.350
which are patient
ID and vaccine ID.

113
00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:08.820
It can't depend on just
part of the value,

114
00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:11.405
otherwise this creates
partial dependency.

115
00:04:11.405 --> 00:04:13.540
Let's apply this rule
to find out if it's

116
00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:16.075
true for every non-key column.

117
00:04:16.075 --> 00:04:18.460
How do I check that the
patient with the ID of

118
00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:20.840
50 has taken vaccine 1?

119
00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.425
I check the value of
both the patient ID

120
00:04:23.425 --> 00:04:25.170
and the vaccine ID keys.

121
00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:27.580
The combined value is
the only way to return

122
00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:30.490
the vaccination status value
of a specific patient.

123
00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:32.890
This means that there is a
functional dependency between

124
00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:35.495
the status value and
the primary key value.

125
00:04:35.495 --> 00:04:38.005
But if I just want to find
out the vaccine name,

126
00:04:38.005 --> 00:04:40.270
then I don't need
both combined values.

127
00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:42.130
The only information
I need to return

128
00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:44.740
the vaccine name
is a vaccine ID.

129
00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:46.690
As you learned earlier, this

130
00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:48.560
is called partial dependency.

131
00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.874
This should be avoided
in most instances

132
00:04:50.874 --> 00:04:52.975
as it violates the 2NF rule.

133
00:04:52.975 --> 00:04:56.315
Similarly, if I want to
identify the patient's name,

134
00:04:56.315 --> 00:04:58.385
I don't need both
combined values.

135
00:04:58.385 --> 00:05:00.335
I can just use the patient ID

136
00:05:00.335 --> 00:05:01.960
to return the patient's name.

137
00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:05.690
Next, let's look at how to
upgrade this table to 2NF.

138
00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:08.180
First, I need to make
all non-key columns

139
00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.735
dependent on all components
of the primary key.

140
00:05:10.735 --> 00:05:13.340
I identify the entities

141
00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:15.440
included in the
vaccination table.

142
00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:17.150
In this instance there are

143
00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:19.535
three entities:
Vaccination status,

144
00:05:19.535 --> 00:05:21.500
as represented by
the status column,

145
00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:22.850
vaccine, which is

146
00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:25.220
the vaccine ID and
vaccine name columns,

147
00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:26.960
and patient, represented by

148
00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:29.600
the patient name and
patient ID columns.

149
00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:31.280
I then break up the table into

150
00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:34.175
three separate tables as
follows: Patient table,

151
00:05:34.175 --> 00:05:37.325
vaccine table, and
vaccination status.

152
00:05:37.325 --> 00:05:39.530
Now in each of these new tables,

153
00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:41.420
all non-primary key attributes

154
00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:43.810
depend only on the
primary key value.

155
00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:46.550
I've eliminated all
unnecessary replication of

156
00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:47.810
the vaccine and patient

157
00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:49.925
names within the
vaccination table.

158
00:05:49.925 --> 00:05:51.620
The three tables are now in

159
00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:54.955
the second normal form, or 2NF.

160
00:05:54.955 --> 00:05:58.220
You should now be familiar
with the 2NF rule and how to

161
00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:02.250
upgrade a table to
2NF. Good work.